#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175679 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/id #uuid "7a8775a6-f16c-5fc8-865b-89728d73a2c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-213" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t) = -\\frac{1}{3}t^3 + \\frac{5}{2}t^2 + 6t + 1,\\quad t\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary, and give the displacement of the particle from the origin at this time." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}+6t+1) \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}+5t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\, \\textrm{m/s}$. So, we set $v = 0$ and solve for $t \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-{t}^{2}+5t+6 \\\\ 0 & ={t}^{2}-5t-6 \\\\ 0 & =(t-6)(t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=6$ and $t=-1.$ Since $t\\geq 0,$ we discard the solution $t=-1$ and keep only $t=6.$\n\nSo the particle is stationary only at $t=6\\, \\textrm{s}.$ To find the displacement from the origin at this time, we substitute $t=6$ back into $s(t)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {6}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}+6\\cdot 6+1 \\\\  & =-72+90+36+1 \\\\  & =55\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t = 6\\, \\textrm{s}$ and its displacement from the origin at this time is $55\\, \\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175680 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "a6a4547d-a81a-5c9a-9ab1-2bb20d847fca" :question/math-academy-id "e-217" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t) = t^3-3t^2+12t,\\quad t\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+12t) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-6t+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-6t+12) \\\\  & =6t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6t-6 \\\\ 6t & =6 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1$. To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1) = 3(1)^2 - 6(1)+12 = 9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/id #uuid "95ddf955-ea59-5ac8-977d-767e46b92bf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6159" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t) = t^4 - 32t$, where $t \\geq 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-32t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t) <    0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}-32 & <0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3} & <32 \\\\ {t}^{3} & <8 \\\\ t & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in\\left[ 0, 2\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d20b8dd4-3ddd-55b3-86fb-83375e88024f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313226} {:answer/id #uuid "a41cbfb8-13e7-5a6a-a618-8f4d2b734f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313227} {:answer/id #uuid "82692d2c-fd1e-5b01-b3cb-6c8a0df337ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313228} {:answer/id #uuid "e2935a20-1892-5012-9267-697c870c8e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313229} {:answer/id #uuid "327e9d84-65fe-5e64-abf1-9e0e1ec85e54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{2}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a41cbfb8-13e7-5a6a-a618-8f4d2b734f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313227} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bdadce8-c65a-5ba9-962b-2b2deff8f5d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50f47d99-0d29-59c2-bb2e-982faa827891" :question/math-academy-id "q-109289" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}} & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2 & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & <-2 \\\\ t & <{e}^{-2} \\\\ t & <\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82e30da0-c514-5d6b-a4f2-32b57f53eeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313233} {:answer/id #uuid "feb1851d-1cdc-5e66-b8be-5e49f929eb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313234} {:answer/id #uuid "13ed8951-f25a-51bc-80b8-2224b159127c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313235} {:answer/id #uuid "c3741a95-9af5-5d6c-82c3-c2495ede1033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313236} {:answer/id #uuid "0b552bf9-1beb-5221-a168-63e371ef621b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b552bf9-1beb-5221-a168-63e371ef621b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313237} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b305db0-a24f-59f4-9972-7b09dd35b380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2912d1ed-8c93-531d-8d23-8555cf0ff32d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109290" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=t{e}^{-t},$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-t} & <0 \\\\ 1-t & <0 \\\\ t & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "808c8f3d-93e7-599a-a6d0-425650200bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2}, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175045} {:answer/id #uuid "db1dd33b-e016-5116-aec2-6632f88be070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 2)$ and $(3, ∞)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175046} {:answer/id #uuid "f6938bc7-5ce8-53a8-aa58-ed003e23859e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175047} {:answer/id #uuid "fd749b77-a715-5978-94b8-7eb25671717b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175048} {:answer/id #uuid "239e3522-01f2-5fe1-8fad-d3d7d96ff819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1)$ and $(5, ∞)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd749b77-a715-5978-94b8-7eb25671717b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175048} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0e2f1f-4609-5672-adf9-8aef0c1741d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175044}] :question/id #uuid "2e226491-3c89-5e59-9e2d-888677b66097" :question/math-academy-id "q-48909" :question/problem "The displacement $s$, in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t) = (3t - 5)^{2}$, where $t > 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64a51385-11d7-51c7-a2d4-7659fb60ca51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,s=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293192} {:answer/id #uuid "e5251bd4-427b-5273-8a5d-2b9528d5d26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,s=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293193} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceb25f5-097c-520e-aaf4-5ab806fd9f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,s=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293194} {:answer/id #uuid "55b41eab-0494-56bc-92fe-c4dd35e7ce67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,s=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293195} {:answer/id #uuid "7b258107-4669-57fb-b219-823bab2c3396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,s=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55b41eab-0494-56bc-92fe-c4dd35e7ce67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,s=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293195} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d351ef-39f4-5dae-94d8-3aeaa48f46a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2ee9a7a-96f8-5c1a-98bc-31a0726aad61" :question/math-academy-id "q-73188" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}-2t+8,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time when the particle is stationary, and give the displacement of the particle from the origin at this time." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+8) \\\\  & =2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-2 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =2 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the particle is stationary only at $t=1.$ To find the displacement from the origin at this time, we substitute $t=1$ back into $s(t)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(1) & ={1}^{2}-2(1)+8 \\\\  & =1-2+8 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=1$ and its displacement from the origin at this time is $7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ff6c60-0496-59ed-8942-e2c8279a45e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273407} {:answer/id #uuid "ac445ed8-952c-5de1-a6c0-5dc70b0c8824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)-2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273408} {:answer/id #uuid "0804e507-30be-5121-94cc-9076f9654b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)+1$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273409} {:answer/id #uuid "8eaa79a7-0886-5ca5-aa46-640b99b4fc38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)+2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273410} {:answer/id #uuid "200b4d45-b33d-5820-8836-b437c8a6186d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $(2)-1$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41ff6c60-0496-59ed-8942-e2c8279a45e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "time s = $2$; velocity m per s = $-1/e^2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273407} :answer-field/id #uuid "f80de19d-031f-542d-9602-c6d7f9e5629d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9016b9c4-555f-5f54-8e92-6ab14881289d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73243" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t{e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((1-t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-2){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(1-(2)){e}^{-(2)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8155132e-fb38-5ac5-a729-ddd6db4a43fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313252} {:answer/id #uuid "69b7e53b-44b6-5593-8a6e-deb2504add7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313253} {:answer/id #uuid "df7e3861-6845-507f-a3fe-2ab1edef4223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313254} {:answer/id #uuid "38f0227d-9324-56e2-bff3-5e9ab523f2f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313255} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9e018a-54ab-5d05-8046-62e2477ef1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b9e018a-54ab-5d05-8046-62e2477ef1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313256} :answer-field/id #uuid "bee01c62-f6b0-5470-ac4f-67bfcd38591e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f742d5d3-d314-55a4-a2e7-77b78815a252" :question/math-academy-id "q-73335" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(t+1){e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-1){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-1 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=-1\\cdot {e}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$"}]}
